<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-euler-lagrange" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Calculus of Variations Formulation of the Euler-Lagrange Equation</title>

  <definition xml:id="def-euler-lagrange">
    <title>The Euler-Lagrange Equation</title>
    <idx>euler-lagrange equation</idx>

    <statement>
      <p>
        For a functional <m>J[y] := \int_{x_1}^{x_2}{F\big(x,y(x),y_x(x)\big) \, dx}</m>, the <term>Euler-Lagrange Equation</term> is
      </p>
      <md>
        \frac{\partial F}{\partial x} - \frac{d}{dx} \big( \frac{\partial F}{\partial y_x} \big) = 0 \, .
      </md>
    </statement>

    <remark>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Euler-Lagrange equation provides the necessary conditions for extremizing a
          <xref ref="def-path" text="custom">path</xref>. However, it does not
          provide details on the nature of the extremum (e.g., if it is a minimum, maximum, or saddle point).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </remark>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <xref ref="def-stationary" text="custom">stationary</xref> action
        <xref ref="def-path" text="custom">paths</xref> derived from the Euler-Lagrange equation can alternatively
        be expressed in Hamiltonian phase space via
        <xref ref="def-legendre-transform" text="custom">Legendre transforms</xref>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </remark>

</subsection>